WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.942
Passwords, API keys, connection strings, and signing keys.

00:00:04.942 --> 00:00:09.623
Almost every Orchard Core site depends on sensitive values like these.

00:00:09.623 --> 00:00:12.827
The Secrets module gives them one secure home.

00:00:12.827 --> 00:00:17.261
In this video, we'll see why the Secrets module exists, how to use

00:00:17.261 --> 00:00:21.450
each of its features, and how to adopt it in your own modules.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:30.499
Without a central place for them, each feature keeps its own credentials in

00:00:30.499 --> 00:00:33.190
its own settings, protected in its own way.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:37.420
Nothing lists them, so it's hard to know which credentials a site uses,

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:40.880
which ones expire soon, or which ones need to be rotated.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.569
A value can't be shared between features, and it can't be kept in

00:00:45.569 --> 00:00:48.254
a dedicated vault, such as Azure Key Vault.

00:00:48.254 --> 00:00:52.910
And moving a site to another environment means entering every credential again.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:55.778
The Secrets module solves this.

00:00:55.778 --> 00:00:59.969
Secrets are encrypted, listed in one place, kept in the store of your

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:03.824
choice, and referenced by name from the settings that use them.

00:01:03.824 --> 00:01:05.165
And it's optional:

00:01:05.165 --> 00:01:07.680
every feature keeps working without it.

00:01:11.868 --> 00:01:14.613
The Secrets module comes with three features.

00:01:14.613 --> 00:01:16.928
Let's search the features for secret.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:19.422
Secrets is the core feature.

00:01:19.422 --> 00:01:25.610
It encrypts secrets with ASP.NET Core Data Protection, and keeps them in the database by default.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:32.010
Secrets, Azure Key Vault, adds another store, which keeps secrets in an Azure Key Vault.

00:01:32.462 --> 00:01:37.802
And OpenID Connect Secrets lets the OpenID Connect server sign and encrypt its

00:01:37.802 --> 00:01:40.282
tokens with keys kept as secrets.

00:01:40.732 --> 00:01:42.606
Let's enable Secrets.

00:01:42.606 --> 00:01:45.522
Its pages are under Tools, Security:

00:01:45.522 --> 00:01:48.992
Secrets, Migrate Secrets, and Secret Stores.

00:01:53.193 --> 00:01:57.583
On the Secrets page, Add Secret first asks for the type of secret.

00:01:58.033 --> 00:02:02.454
A text secret holds a value, like a password or an API key.

00:02:02.454 --> 00:02:06.058
An RSA key holds a key pair, generated for you.

00:02:06.058 --> 00:02:12.723
And an X.509 certificate references a certificate from the certificate store of the machine.

00:02:13.174 --> 00:02:16.265
Let's create a text secret for a payment API key.

00:02:16.265 --> 00:02:20.424
Its name is how settings reference it, so it can't be changed later.

00:02:20.878 --> 00:02:25.431
The value is encrypted before it's saved, and it's never displayed again.

00:02:25.431 --> 00:02:28.658
A description tells others what the secret is for.

00:02:29.109 --> 00:02:31.191
The expiration date is optional.

00:02:31.191 --> 00:02:33.163
It doesn't disable the secret:

00:02:33.163 --> 00:02:35.519
it's a reminder of when to rotate it.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:40.890
The secret is listed with badges for its type, its store, and its expiration.

00:02:45.072 --> 00:02:51.482
As the list grows, the search box finds secrets by name, description, type, or store.

00:02:51.932 --> 00:02:57.014
And the filters show the secrets that have expired, expire within 30 days,

00:02:57.014 --> 00:03:00.572
or never expire, as well as the secrets of a type.

00:03:01.028 --> 00:03:03.018
Expired secrets keep working:

00:03:03.018 --> 00:03:06.372
the warning is there so that you rotate them in time.

00:03:06.372 --> 00:03:09.328
Administrators also see it on their dashboard.

00:03:09.778 --> 00:03:11.875
To rotate a secret, open it.

00:03:11.875 --> 00:03:13.972
The value field stays empty.

00:03:13.972 --> 00:03:18.968
Enter a new value to replace it, or leave it empty to keep the current one.

00:03:19.419 --> 00:03:24.169
To delete several secrets at once, select them, and use the Actions menu.

00:03:28.359 --> 00:03:34.204
Features that need a credential, like the SMTP email settings, all use the same editor.

00:03:34.204 --> 00:03:37.346
By default, it holds a user-specified value:

00:03:37.346 --> 00:03:43.379
the value is encrypted, and kept with the settings, exactly as without the Secrets module.

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:46.930
Choose Use a secret, and pick one from the list.

00:03:47.382 --> 00:03:51.982
If the secret doesn't exist yet, New secret opens the secret editor in

00:03:51.982 --> 00:03:54.342
a new tab, with a suggested name.

00:03:54.792 --> 00:03:56.832
Enter the value, and save it.

00:04:01.895 --> 00:04:06.603
Back in the settings, the reload button refreshes the list, and selects the

00:04:06.603 --> 00:04:09.915
new secret, without losing the changes on the page.

00:04:10.366 --> 00:04:16.462
Save the settings, and SMTP now reads its password from the Secrets module.

00:04:16.462 --> 00:04:22.935
The email, SMS, Meta, GitHub, Google, Microsoft, X, OpenID Connect, and Azure AI

00:04:22.935 --> 00:04:25.946
Search settings all work this way.

00:04:30.113 --> 00:04:33.614
Most sites already have credentials saved in their settings.

00:04:33.614 --> 00:04:38.973
Migrate Secrets lists every credential that's still kept in the settings of an enabled feature.

00:04:39.429 --> 00:04:41.047
Select the ones to move.

00:04:41.047 --> 00:04:44.229
Each one gets a suggested name, which you can change.

00:04:44.679 --> 00:04:49.118
Move to Secrets decrypts each value, saves it as a secret, and updates

00:04:49.118 --> 00:04:51.162
the settings to reference it.

00:04:51.162 --> 00:04:56.009
An existing secret is never overwritten, and a credential that can't be moved

00:04:56.009 --> 00:04:57.469
stays where it was.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:02.536
The credentials are now secrets, and the features that use them keep working,

00:05:02.536 --> 00:05:04.260
without any other change.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:12.459
To keep secrets in Azure Key Vault, register a Secret Client in the

00:05:12.459 --> 00:05:16.732
host, and select it in the configuration of the Azure Key Vault feature.

00:05:16.732 --> 00:05:19.690
Each tenant gets its own namespace in the vault.

00:05:21.262 --> 00:05:24.452
Then enable Secrets, Azure Key Vault.

00:05:25.931 --> 00:05:29.171
When you create a secret, you can now choose its store.

00:05:36.761 --> 00:05:39.487
The store badge shows where each secret is kept.

00:05:39.487 --> 00:05:44.131
Settings reference secrets by name, so they don't need to know which store keeps them.

00:05:48.323 --> 00:05:51.853
Secret Stores moves secrets from one store to another.

00:05:51.853 --> 00:05:56.383
Select the source store, and it shows how many active secrets it keeps.

00:05:56.833 --> 00:05:59.718
Choose the destination store, and confirm.

00:05:59.718 --> 00:06:04.346
Each secret is copied, verified, and only then removed from the source.

00:06:04.346 --> 00:06:08.493
Names don't change, so the settings that use them keep working.

00:06:08.944 --> 00:06:13.864
And to move a single secret, use Move, next to it, on the Secrets list.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:23.586
With OpenID Connect Secrets enabled, the OpenID Connect server can sign and encrypt

00:06:23.586 --> 00:06:29.630
its tokens with RSA keys kept as secrets, instead of certificates installed on the machine.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:32.799
Select the keys in the server settings.

00:06:32.799 --> 00:06:37.330
Every instance of the site then uses the same keys, wherever it runs.

00:06:41.527 --> 00:06:44.208
Deployment plans can include secrets.

00:06:44.208 --> 00:06:49.383
The Secrets step exports their names and metadata, and their values only when

00:06:49.383 --> 00:06:53.747
they're encrypted with an RSA key that the target site also has.

00:06:55.347 --> 00:06:59.349
And the Secrets recipe step creates secrets when a site is set up,

00:06:59.349 --> 00:07:04.407
with values read from the configuration, such as environment variables, so they never

00:07:04.407 --> 00:07:05.907
appear in the recipe.

00:07:10.107 --> 00:07:14.996
Your own modules can offer the same experience, without depending on the Secrets module.

00:07:14.996 --> 00:07:18.949
Keep the protected value in your settings, add a property for the name

00:07:18.949 --> 00:07:22.277
of a secret, and edit both with a secret input view model.

00:07:23.077 --> 00:07:27.780
In the display driver, create the editor model from the stored values, and

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:30.425
apply the posted one with Update Async.

00:07:30.425 --> 00:07:33.657
It validates the input, and protects a new value.

00:07:34.457 --> 00:07:37.462
Render it with the secret input tag helper.

00:07:37.462 --> 00:07:42.305
At runtime, Get Secret Value Async reads the secret when one is selected,

00:07:42.305 --> 00:07:44.757
and the protected value otherwise.

00:07:45.557 --> 00:07:50.766
Optionally, a display driver lists your credentials on the Migrate Secrets screen.

00:07:50.766 --> 00:07:54.967
The documentation of the Secrets module walks through every step.

00:07:56.967 --> 00:07:58.672
That's the Secrets module:

00:07:58.672 --> 00:08:02.507
one secure home for the sensitive values of your site, kept in the

00:08:02.507 --> 00:08:06.023
store of your choice, and easy to adopt in your own modules.

00:08:06.023 --> 00:08:07.407
Thanks for watching.

